    "https://stedwards.applicantpro.com/jobs/4250807": {
      "title": "Assistant Director, HEAL Project",
      "description": "Assistant Director, HEAL Project. St Edwards University. Assistant Director of the HEAL Project Office of Partnerships and Professional Studies, Academic Affairs Salary Range: $82,000-$85,000 St. Edward's University of Austin, Texas invites applications for the position of Assistant Director of the HEAL Project. This is a grant funded position. Continued employment is contingent upon the ongoing availability of grant funds from the Federal Government. Federal grant funding allows for a newly created position, the Assistant Director of the HEAL project, under the Office of Partnerships and Professional Studies (OPPS). The role will focus on supporting the operations, management, and partnership development of the Central Texas Healthcare Education and Advancement of Learning (HEAL) project at St. Edward's University. This is a transformative curricular and workforce development project that seeks to expand the University's academic capabilities serving low-income, alternative, and non-traditional adult learners in need of accessible pathways for career advancement in healthcare. HEAL was designed in response to clinical shortages across the Central Texas region, and the project collaborates with a variety of regional healthcare and workforce partners to help establish accelerated, stackable, and portable healthcare workforce credentials. The Assistant Director will support the Director across the project's build, implementation, partnership development, and reporting phases. The role will work closely with the School of Health Sciences and other centralized university departments. Minimum Qualifications: Must have a bachelor's degree in Higher Education Administration, Education, Public Health, Curriculum and Instruction, or other related areas, alongside experience managing complex educational projects or federal grants. Demonstrated experience in project management, managing instructional design timelines or program/product development milestones, and working with workforce development boards/agencies. An interest and background in solving complex, creative problems in Education, Healthcare, or corporate environments for learners and employees. Comfortability working in collaborative, mission-focused, and entrepreneurial environments. Experience interpreting federal higher education rules, managing contracts, or dealing with state regulatory commissions. Preferred Qualifications: A graduate degree in a related field to Higher Education Administration, Education, Public Health, Curriculum and Instruction, or other related areas, alongside a minimum of 3 years of experience managing complex educational projects or federal grants. At least 3 years of Higher Education administration experience. Knowledge, Skills & Abilities Experience being adaptable in the face of complex project problems and timelines. Experience and comfortability in external facing roles and presenting information to groups of people. General knowledge of course design principles and modern innovations in teaching and learning. General knowledge and ability to work with university systems, including but not limited to Microsoft suite, Google suite, any learning management system, and any SIS/CRM. Working knowledge and application of chat/LLM prompting. Ability to manage multiple priorities in a fast-paced, team-oriented environment. Demonstrate strong communication skills and a solid background in the English language. Essential Duties and Responsibilities Include: Regulatory Compliance & Partnerships: Monitor the Department of Education's (DOE) rulemaking and regulatory changes concerning Workforce Pell Grant guidelines in anticipation of project eligibility, alongside relevant guidelines from the Texas Higher Education Coordinating Board (THECB). Federal and State guidelines for curricular development will support broader OPPS infrastructure and professional programs. Manage contacts and relationships with the Project's associated workforce board partners and employer partners as needed. Curriculum Coordination: Support and contribute to the project's instructional design process with the School of Health Sciences. Coordinate contracts and timelines with the subject matter experts and instructional designers to ensure all grant funded healthcare programs meet curricular best practices and innovative deliveries/modalities for enrolled learners. Personnel Management: Prepare for and lead the onboarding and management of the project's Workforce Success Coach and the Workforce Partnerships Specialist in future grant years. Systems Integration: Collaborate with centralized university operations and staff to ensure learner applications, student information systems, and instructional support technologies provide a seamless learner journey. The student journey framework will support both the Project and serve as an underlying scalable model for broader applications across OPPS. Project Vision: Support the HEAL Project's overall growth, considering its impacts as a scalable program framework for OPPS that goes above and beyond the School of Health Sciences. Other duties as assigned, trained for and qualified to do. Supervisory Responsibilities None in Year 1 of the grant funding period For detailed information, please scroll to the bottom of the page to download the job description. The University offers an excellent TOTAL REWARDS package! Medical & Rx Coverage (HSA Available) Dental Vision Flexible Spending Accounts Short Term Disability (STD) Insurance Long Term Disability (LTD) Insurance Life & Accidental Death & Dismemberment (AD&D) Insurance Employee Assistance Program (EAP) Pet Insurance Annual Leave & Paid Sick Leave Retirement Plan – (TIAA) Employee 5%/Employer Match 7% Tuition Benefits Paid Holidays Services & Discounts How to Apply Interested applicants should submit an online application at; https://stedwards.applicantpro.com . Please include resume, cover letter, and three employment references . Applicants should submit materials for full consideration. Mission Integration Statement St. Edward's was founded by the Congregation of Holy Cross, from which it acquired distinguishing characteristics that include the courage to take risks, an international perspective and the commitment to provide educational opportunities for students of varied cultural, religious, educational and economic backgrounds. In support of the mission, the different backgrounds of St. Edward's students, faculty and staff enrich our community and represent a significant part of our culture and commitment to excellence. We value the individual differences, life experiences, unique capabilities and innovative talent that our employees and students contribute to our vibrant academic environment. St. Edward's honors and respects all members of its community by fostering an inclusive and welcoming environment that respects the dignity and worth of each person and stresses the obligation of all people to pursue a more just world. About St. Edward's University St. Edward's University is a private, Catholic liberal arts institution of more than 3000 students located in Austin, Texas. St. Edward's emphasizes critical thinking and ethical practices, as well as small classes, personalized learning and exciting internship opportunities. The community atmosphere extends to the approximately 650 faculty and staff who work together to make the university a welcoming yet challenging environment for students. It is an exciting time to be part of the St. Edward's community as the university increases its global engagement to educate students about the opportunities and challenges of a 21st-century world. SEU is a diverse community that appreciates faculty and students from all backgrounds. More information about St. Edward's University may be found at www.stedwards.edu . Equal Opportunity Employer St. Edward's University, as an equal opportunity employer, complies with all applicable federal and state laws regarding nondiscrimination. The University is committed to a policy of equal opportunity for all persons and does not discriminate on the basis of race, color, national origin, age, marital status, sex, sexual orientation, gender identity, gender expression, disability, religion, or veteran status in employment, educational programs and activities, and admissions. We require U.S. citizenship or permanent residency for employment, and we are not offering visa sponsorship at this time. Background Checks: A criminal history background check is required for the finalist(s) under consideration for this position."
    },
    "https://stedwards.applicantpro.com/jobs/4250691": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor of Kinesiology (tenure-track)",
      "description": "Assistant Professor of Kinesiology (tenure-track). St Edwards University. Assistant Professor (tenure-track) Kinesiology Department St. Edward's University, a nationally ranked, independent Catholic university and Hispanic Serving Institution (HSI), invites applications for a full-time, 9-month, tenure-track position as an assistant professor in the Department of Kinesiology, beginning August 2027. St. Edward's is characterized by its commitment to the Holy Cross educational mission of educating the hearts and minds of a diverse student body deeply committed to social justice. Teaching is central to the mission of St. Edward's University, and the teaching load for this position is 12 credits per semester. New tenure-track faculty receive one course reassignment per year for research. Courses are delivered face-to-face. The successful candidate will be expected to teach courses such as Exercise Physiology; Strength Training and Conditioning; Musculoskeletal Anatomy and Biomechanics; and Exercise Testing and Prescription. Candidates may also be expected to teach Anatomy and Physiology I as well as Anatomy and Physiology II. For consideration, candidates much possess an earned doctorate in exercise physiology, exercise science, kinesiology or related area. QUALIFICATIONS Demonstrated experience in college-level teaching and scholarship required; Excellent written and spoken English communication skills; A strong commitment to and demonstrated potential for excellence in teaching and training undergraduates from diverse backgrounds; Excellent interpersonal and communication skills, and demonstrated ability to work collaboratively; Successful completion of employment and/or criminal background check is required. Essential Responsibilities Include: Candidates should have an interest in and the ability or potential to teach undergraduate courses, as well as support a diverse student body. In addition, successful candidates are expected to: Academically advise undergraduate students Meet school and university service requirements Enthusiastically participate in promoting the kinesiology major Maintain an active program of quality research Develop new courses and/or curriculum, as requested Maintain commitment to social responsibility in the classroom setting in line with the University's mission Other duties as assigned HOW TO APPLY Interested applicants should submit an online application by clicking \"Apply for this Position\". Application packages should include: Cover letter Curriculum vitae Names and contact information for three references Mission Integration Statement (see next section for details) All faculty applications must include a statement outlining how they would integrate the University's Holy Cross mission into their work. Your Mission Integration statement should be included as a separate document. Mission Integration Statement Prompt: St. Edward's was founded by the Congregation of Holy Cross, from which it acquired distinguishing characteristics that include the courage to take risks, an international perspective, and a commitment to providing educational opportunities for students from varied cultural, religious, educational, and economic backgrounds. In support of the mission, the different backgrounds of St. Edward's students, faculty, and staff enrich our community and represent a significant part of our culture and commitment to excellence. We value the individual differences, life experiences, unique capabilities, and innovative talent that our employees and students contribute to our vibrant academic environment. St. Edward's honors and respects all members of its community by fostering an inclusive and welcoming environment that values the dignity and worth of each person and emphasizes the obligation of all individuals to work towards a more just world. While serving as a faculty member at St. Edward's University, how would your individual differences, life experiences, and unique capabilities contribute to the mission as described above? Describe how your prior work aligns with the university's mission. Applicants should submit materials by December 1, 2026, for full consideration; however, the position will remain open until filled. Incomplete applications will not receive full consideration."
    },
    "https://stedwards.applicantpro.com/jobs/4250757": {
      "title": "Tenure Track Assistant Professor Of Chemistry (Analytical)",
      "description": "Tenure Track Assistant Professor Of Chemistry (Analytical). St Edwards University. Tenure Track Assistant Professor -Analytical Department of Chemistry St. Edward's University, a nationally ranked, independent Catholic university and Hispanic Serving Institution (HSI) invites applications for a full-time, 9-month, tenure-track position as an Assistant Professor in the Department of Chemistry, beginning August 2027. St. Edward's is characterized by its commitment to the Holy Cross educational mission to educate the hearts and minds of a diverse student body that is deeply committed to social justice. The successful candidate will have expertise in Analytical Chemistry, and a passion for teaching and mentoring undergraduate research. The Chemistry Department offers B.A. and B.S. degrees in Chemistry and a B.S. in Biochemistry, and is part of the School of Natural Sciences. For consideration, a Ph.D. in Chemistry is required. Postdoctoral and undergraduate teaching experience is preferred. Demonstrated potential for excellence in undergraduate teaching and engaging in research with undergraduates is required. Experience or interest in pedagogical innovation and implementing active learning in their courses is desired. Candidates with research experience that would enhance our current chemistry degree offerings are particularly encouraged to apply. We seek a colleague who will align with and expand St. Edward's University mission, values, and strategic plan. Successful completion of an employment and/or criminal history background check is required. Essential Responsibilities Include: Teaching the equivalent of four 3-credit courses each semester (with an automatic one course reduction for research for new tenure-track faculty). Courses may include General Chemistry I & II lecture/labs, Analytical Chemistry lecture/lab, and Instrumental Analysis lecture/lab. Developing elective courses in the candidate's area of expertise. Developing a successful research program involving undergraduates and compatible with resources available at a primarily undergraduate liberal arts institution. Participation in service to the department, school and university. Academic advising of chemistry majors. Other duties as assigned. HOW TO APPLY Interested applicants should submit an online application by clicking \"Apply for this Position\". Application packages should include: Cover letter Curriculum vitae Names and contact information for three references Statement describing your teaching philosophy Statement about your approach to working on research projects with undergraduate students Additionally, all faculty applications must include a statement outlining how they would integrate the University's Holy Cross mission into their work. Your Mission Integration statement should be included as a separate document. Mission Integration Statement Prompt: St. Edward's was founded by the Congregation of Holy Cross, from which it acquired distinguishing characteristics that include the courage to take risks, an international perspective, and a commitment to providing educational opportunities for students from varied cultural, religious, educational, and economic backgrounds. In support of the mission, the different backgrounds of St. Edward's students, faculty, and staff enrich our community and represent a significant part of our culture and commitment to excellence. We value the individual differences, life experiences, unique capabilities, and innovative talent that our employees and students contribute to our vibrant academic environment. St. Edward's honors and respects all members of its community by fostering an inclusive and welcoming environment that values the dignity and worth of each person and emphasizes the obligation of all individuals to work towards a more just world. While serving as a faculty member at St. Edward's University, how would your individual differences, life experiences, and unique capabilities contribute to the mission as described above? Describe how your prior work aligns with the university's mission. Applicants should submit materials by December 1, 2026 for full consideration; however, the position will remain open until filled."
    },
    "https://stedwards.applicantpro.com/jobs/4259527": {
      "title": "Campus Security Officer",
      "description": "Campus Security Officer. St Edwards University. Campus Security Officer University Police Department St. Edward's University of Austin, Texas invites applications for the position of Campus Security Officer. The Campus Security Officer provides a safe and secure environment for students, faculty, staff, and visitors to the main campus. Facilitates and encourages respect for the law, rules and procedures, safety and institutional policy. The person selected works to educate students, faculty, staff and visitors, regarding safety and security, minimize risk for the University and enforce all University policies and procedures. For consideration, High school diploma or equivalent required. An employee in this position must be 21 years or older at time of employment; a U.S. citizen, have a valid Texas Driver's License, and have a good driving record. Current licensed security officer in good standing. Minimum 2 years' experience in safety or security. Experience in college or University setting preferred. Not currently under indictment for any criminal offense; Never been convicted of an offense above a Class C misdemeanor Never been convicted of any family violence offense; Not prohibited by state or federal law from operating a motor vehicle; The preferred candidate will have competency in the use of MS Word and MS Excel and be able to learn other equipment and programs. Prior parking enforcement experience preferred. Must be possess good organizational skills, maintain high quality control standards. Successfully complete extensive background check, physical test and drug screening. Ability to learn St. Edward's University policies. Ability to walk for long periods in all weather conditions. Ability to sit, stand, walk, and lift 50 pounds. Ability to write legibly and accurately record information. Ability to complete work assignments, take and understand direction. Must be able to handle crisis situations in a calm and effective manner. Ability to communicate effectively both written and verbally. Ability to establish and maintain effective and appropriate relationships with the St. Edward's University community and visitors. Essential Responsibilities Include: The Campus Security Officer (officer) performs vehicle and foot patrols of the campus, parking lots, garage and buildings to monitor behavior, secure buildings and property, maintain order and ensure rule compliance, and enforce regulations. The officer monitors the physical safety of all buildings by locking and unlocking doors, patrolling or monitoring, and reporting suspicious behavior. The officer may also be tasked with enforcing traffic and parking regulations. This position has regular interaction with the general public. The officer greets students, faculty, staff and visitors, familiarizes them with the rules and regulations of the campus, helps direct them to specific locations or offices as needed, and answers other questions. This position is considered security sensitive and will involve shift work, special events, days, evenings, and overnight as required. The Campus Security Officer will be expected to represent St. Edward's University and the University Police Department in a positive and professional manner and to promote a safe and secure campus environment. This person is required to patrol on foot, golf cart or other University vehicle. This person will be subject to an extensive background investigation, fingerprinting, medical, physical and driving history check . For detailed information, please scroll to the bottom of the page to download the job description. The University offers an excellent TOTAL REWARDS package! Medical & Rx Coverage Dental Vision Short Term Disability (STD) Insurance Long Term Disability (LTD) Insurance Life & Accidental Death & Dismemberment (AD&D) Insurance Employee Assistance Program (EAP) Pet Insurance Annual Leave & Paid Sick Leave Retirement Plan – (TIAA) Employee 5%/Employer Match 7% Tuition Benefits Paid Holidays Services & Discounts HOW TO APPLY Interested applicants should submit an online application at; https://stedwards.applicantpro.com . Please include resume, cover letter, and three employment references . No Calls Please. Applications will not be considered if it is missing any of these three items. In your cover letter, please describe your lived experiences that prepare you to contribute to diversity and inclusion at St. Edward's University? EQUAL OPPORTUNITY EMPLOYER: St. Edward's University, as an equal opportunity/affirmative action employer, complies with all applicable federal and state laws regarding nondiscrimination and affirmative action. The University is committed to a policy of equal opportunity for all persons and does not discriminate on the basis of race, color, national origin, age, marital status, sex, sexual orientation, gender identity, gender expression, disability, religion, or veteran status in employment, educational programs and activities, and admissions. ABOUT ST. EDWARD'S UNIVERSITY Founded in 1885 by the Congregation of Holy Cross, St. Edward's University is a private, Catholic liberal arts institution of more than 4,600 diverse students located in Austin, Texas. St. Edward's emphasizes critical thinking and ethical practices, as well as small classes, personalized learning and exciting internship opportunities. The community atmosphere extends to the approximately 800 faculty and staff who work together to make the University a welcoming yet challenging environment for students. An overview of St. Edward's University employee benefits is available at; https://www.stedwards.edu/human-resources/benefits-summary Sponsorship: We are not offering sponsorship at this time. Background Checks: A criminal history background check is required for finalist(s) under consideration for this position. Reference #:J2026074 Available: Immediately"
    },
    "https://stedwards.applicantpro.com/jobs/4259515": {
      "title": "Graduate Admission Recruiter",
      "description": "Graduate Admission Recruiter. St Edwards University. Graduate Admission Recruiter Enrollment Management St. Edward's University of Austin, Texas invites applications for the position of Graduate Admission Recruiter. The position will recruit qualified prospective students for graduate programs at St. Edward's University. Serving as the primary contact from initial inquiry through enrollment, this role delivers high-touch, consultative guidance to help working professionals and adult learners explore academic opportunities, navigate the application process, and achieve their goals. The Graduate Admission Recruiter manages full applicant funnels across assigned territories or student segments, executing multi-channel outreach strategies (phone, text, email, events). By collaborating with faculty, academic leadership, and external partners, this position elevates the reputation of St. Edward's graduate offerings in the Munday School of Business, School of Health Sciences and the School of Behavioral and Social Sciences and contributes to institutional growth in alignment with our Holy Cross mission. For consideration, Bachelor's degree is required, Master's degree preferred with a minimum of 1–3 years of experience in higher education recruitment/admissions, corporate talent acquisition, consultative sales, or customer service. Ideal candidates will have experience recruiting prospective graduate students for master's or doctoral programs and demonstrated experience with Slate, Salesforce or similar higher education CRM platforms. Essential Responsibilities Include: Prospective Student Engagement & Consultative Outreach Serve as the primary point of contact for prospective graduate students across all university graduate programs. Execute multi-channel follow-up strategies (phone, text, email) and active outreach campaigns to engage prospective students and drive enrollment outcomes. Conduct high-quality, student-centered, consultative conversations to clarify educational/career goals, address questions or enrollment barriers, and guide candidates through application requirements and timelines. Articulate program curricula, flexible course formats, start terms, and career pathways using approved university resources. Provide clear guidance on affordability, tuition costs, and basic financial aid/scholarship options, referring students to specialized campus offices when detailed support is needed. Pipeline Management & Recruitment Strategy Strategically manage full applicant funnels using Slate (CRM) to track prospective student progress, document communication histories, and schedule timely follow-ups. Analyze territory and lead management data to monitor conversion metrics, speed-to-inquiry, and pipeline progress against team enrollment goals. Review admission applications, monitor candidate completion statuses, and facilitate preliminary evaluation steps to ensure smooth processing. Identify programmatic trends, applicant feedback, and market insights to recommend changes or enhancements to graduate recruitment initiatives. Outreach, Events & Partnership Development Represent St. Edward's University at corporate education events, graduate fairs, community partner events, and professional conferences. Plan and lead virtual information sessions, webinars, and on-campus recruitment events to build program awareness and community engagement. Forge and maintain relationships with local businesses, civic organizations, community colleges, and regional partners to strengthen graduate pipelines. Campus Collaboration & Continuous Improvement Partner closely with graduate program directors, academic deans, faculty, and administrative staff across all schools to align recruitment messaging, streamline admission workflows and relationship building in the community. Participate in regular team and enrollment management meetings to share performance insights and strategies that improve student experiences and operational effectiveness. Perform other duties as assigned, trained for, and qualified to do. For detailed information, please scroll to the bottom of the page to download the job description. The University offers an excellent TOTAL REWARDS package! Medical & Rx Coverage (HSA Available) Dental Vision Flexible Spending Accounts Short Term Disability (STD) Insurance Long Term Disability (LTD) Insurance Life & Accidental Death & Dismemberment (AD&D) Insurance Employee Assistance Program (EAP) Pet Insurance Annual Leave & Paid Sick Leave Retirement Plan – (TIAA) Employee 5%/Employer Match 7% Tuition Benefits Paid Holidays Services & Discounts HOW TO APPLY Interested applicants should submit an online application at; https://stedwards.applicantpro.com . Please include resume, cover letter, and three employment references. No Calls Please. Applications will not be considered if it is missing any of these three items. MISSION INTEGRATION STATEMENT: St. Edward's was founded by the Congregation of Holy Cross, from which it acquired distinguishing characteristics that include the courage to take risks, an international perspective and the commitment to provide educational opportunities for students of varied cultural, religious, educational and economic backgrounds. In support of the mission, the different backgrounds of St. Edward's students, faculty and staff enrich our community and represent a significant part of our culture and commitment to excellence. We value the individual differences, life experiences, unique capabilities and innovative talent that our employees and students contribute to our vibrant academic environment. St. Edward's honors and respects all members of its community by fostering an inclusive and welcoming environment that respects the dignity and worth of each person and stresses the obligation of all people to pursue a more just world. EQUAL OPPORTUNITY EMPLOYER: St. Edward's University, as an equal opportunity/affirmative action employer, complies with all applicable federal and state laws regarding nondiscrimination and affirmative action. The University is committed to a policy of equal opportunity for all persons and does not discriminate on the basis of race, color, national origin, age, marital status, sex, sexual orientation, gender identity, gender expression, disability, religion, or veteran status in employment, educational programs and activities, and admissions. ABOUT ST. EDWARD'S UNIVERSITY Founded in 1885 by the Congregation of Holy Cross, St. Edward's University is a private, Catholic liberal arts institution of more than 4,600 diverse students located in Austin, Texas. St. Edward's emphasizes critical thinking and ethical practices, as well as small classes, personalized learning and exciting internship opportunities. The community atmosphere extends to the approximately 800 faculty and staff who work together to make the university a welcoming yet challenging environment for students. An overview of St. Edward's University employee benefits is available at; https://www.stedwards.edu/human-resources/benefits-summary Sponsorship: We are not offering sponsorship at this time. Background Checks: A criminal history background check is required for finalist(s) under consideration for this position. Reference #:J2026073 Available: Immediately"
